$-------------------------------------HELP
PROMlink cannot detect a programmer connected to this computer.
Possible reasons are:

1)  Incorrect serial ports or cable.  Double-check systems with multiple
    ports and use the cable provided.

2)  Programmer must be switched on and in Computer Remote Control mode.
    This must be selected from the programmer's front panel.

3)  Incompatible serial port parameters.  The default for PROMlink is 9600
    baud, 8 bits, no parity. 

%
~----------1:
Select the function you want by typing the number in ().  For help on
individual menu items, select the function, then type the F3 key.

At any prompt, the F1 key brings you back to the main menu, the F2 key
brings you back to the previous menu and the F3 key brings up the 
corresponding help message screen.

%
~----------2
PROGRAM DEVICE can program the device in the programmer socket or the
device in the handler socket with the data stored in the programmer RAM.

When the programmer programs a device, it actually performs three operations:
1)  An Illegal Bit Test or a Blank Check or no device test according to the
    Device Test displayed in the System Status block.

2)  Programs the device.

3)  Verifies that the data in the device matches the data in RAM.

Before performing handler programming, be sure to set up the handler binning
signals.  Otherwise default values will be used.
%
~----------3
LOAD RAM FROM FILE sends a file through the serial cable to the programmer.

The programmer receives the data using the current I/O format and stores it
in its RAM.  Before performing this operation, be sure that the I/O format
and the device type displayed in the system status block are the ones that
you want to use. 

Load RAM From Current Data File loads RAM with data in the file specified in
the Data File field of the System Status block.  Before initiating this
operation, be sure that the current Data File is the one that you want to use.
%
~----------4
LOAD RAM FROM DEVICE loads programmer RAM with the data in the device 
inserted in the programmer socket.

Before performing Begin Loading, be sure that the Device Type displayed 
in the System Status block is correct.
%
~----------5
CREATE FILE FROM RAM reads programmer RAM into a file on disk.

The format of the file created will be the current I/O format.  Before
performing the operation, be sure the current I/O format is the one desired.

The amount of data stored in the file depends on the I/O Block Size and the
current device size.  A different I/O Block Size can be specified with the
Set Operation Boundaries function.
%
~----------6
SELECT BLANK CHECK/ILLEGAL BIT TEST allows you to select the device test
that will be automatically performed before a device is programmed.  The
Blank Check, Illegal Bit Test or no test may be selected.

If the selected test fails, the devices will not be programmed and an
error message will be displayed.
%
~----------7
VERIFY DEVICE compares the data stored in a device with the data in the
programmer RAM.

When the test is complete, the result will be displayed.  This test does
not affect programmer RAM.

Before performing this operation, check that the current Device Type is
correct.  Also be sure the programmer's RAM contains the data you wish 
to verify.
%
~----------8
FILL RAM fills the programmer RAM with a specified two-digit hexadecimal
number.

You can fill a portion of the RAM by setting the RAM boundaries.
     Begin RAM address specifies the first address of the fill.
     I/O Block Size specifies the number of bytes to be filled or 
     the entire RAM if set to zero.

The FILL RAM command takes almost a full minute to fill 16K of RAM with 
non zero data.
%
~----------9
EDIT/VIEW RAM prompts for a starting RAM address.  Enter this address 
in hexadecimal and hit RETURN.  (If only the RETURN key is entered, the 
starting address will be set to zero.)  A data block of 256 bytes starting
at the specified address will be displayed and put you in the Edit Mode.  
You can use the command keys as shown at the bottom of the data block for
editing programmer RAM on a byte-by-byte basis.

If you exit Edit Mode by pressing F2, you can return to Edit Mode with the
previous starting address unchanged by pressing RETURN only.
%
~----------10
LOAD CONFIGURATION displays the current directory and prompts for a
configuration filename to be loaded.

By typing the name of a previously defined configuration file, you load a new
configuration.

Input format: [d:][path]filename[.ext]
%
~----------11
ERROR LOGGING displays, saves, and clears the programming session error 
counts which are:
     Number of parts correctly programmed
     Number of bad device / continuity errors
     Number of illegal bit / non-blank errors
     Number of programming errors
     Number of data verify errors
     Number of functional verify errors
These numbers start at zero when PROMlink is started, and are updated 
when a device is programmed.  They may be cleared to zero only by 
selecting "Clear Totals" or by exiting PROMlink.

"Save Totals" appends the error counts to a file called STATS.DOC in 
the default directory.  This file is explained in the PROMlink manual.
%
~----------12
SELECT DEVICE TYPE tells the programmer what device you plan to use.

The programmer must know what device you are using in order to correctly 
read or program it.  Your device may be selected from the Select Device 
Type menus or by specifying its Data I/O family/pinout code (found in 
your Data I/O programmer manuals).
%
~----------13
SELECT I/O FORMAT tells the programmer what format your data will be 
in for data transfers between files and programmer RAM.

JEDEC format is not an option on the Select I/O Format menu since the 
I/O format will be set to JEDEC automatically if you select the Model 60
programmer or the LogicPak from the Select Programmer Type menu or the 
Select Pak Type menu.
 
PROMlink performs data transfers by simply copying the data, so the data
format of your files must match that selected at the programmer.  For 
more information on the formats available, consult your programmer manual. 
%
~----------14
SELECT PROGRAMMER TYPE tells PROMlink what programmer configuration you have.

The Select Programmer Type menu has a list of the Data I/O programmers
supported by PROMlink.  Once a programmer type is selected, PROMlink will
try to investigate the RAM size of your programmer.  Be sure to have the
programmer you are going to select ready to communicate with PROMlink so 
that the programmer size displayed is correct. 

If the System 19 or the Model 29 is selected, a second menu will allow 
you to select the Pak type.

If you select the Model 60, the I/O format will be changed to JEDEC 
automatically.  If you begin using any other programmer after using a logic
programmer, PROMlink will default to the Motorola Exormax format.

Error messages may be incorrect if the wrong programmer type is selected.  

Note: The Model 60 and the Model 120 do not allow PROMlink to display RAM
size.
%
~----------15
SET OPERATIONS BOUNDARIES allows you to set the values of the following
parameters:

Begin RAM, I/O Block Size, I/O Offset, Device Block Size, Begin Device,
Bit Mode, Set Size, or to set all these parameters to their defaults.
%
~----------16
BEGIN RAM ADDRESS changes the starting RAM address used by the programmer.
Begin RAM acts like an offset for programmer RAM operations.

This operation affects all subsequent operations which use programmer RAM
(except Edit/View RAM).

The Begin RAM address is entered in hexadecimal or may be changed to its
default value of 0 by pressing the RETURN key. It remains in effect
until changed again or until PROMlink is exited.    
%
~----------17
I/O BLOCK SIZE defines how many bytes are transferred from programmer
RAM when Create File From RAM is performed.

If the I/O Block Size is zero (default), the number of bytes transferred 
will be equal to the number of bytes in the current device type.

I/O Block Size is entered in hexadecimal or may be changed to its default
value of 0 by pressing the RETURN key.  It remains in effect until it is
changed or until PROMlink is exited.  
%
~----------18
I/O OFFSET defines the offset applied to the addresses contained in
data transfer formats.

All data transfers between files and programmer RAM contain addresses as 
part of their format.  When I/O Offset is set to its default value of
FFFFFF hexadecimal, data transfers are as follows:

     Transfer To Programmer - the first format address received is assigned
     to the programmer's Begin RAM address.  All other format addresses are
     assigned to programmer RAM relative to this address.

     Transfer To File - the programmer assigns the data at its Begin RAM
     address to format address 0 and sends format addresses relative to this
     address.

I/O Offset is entered in hexadecimal or may be changed to its default value 
of FFFFFF by pressing the RETURN key. (Note: the System 19 does not have a
default value for I/O Offset, PROMlink sets it to 0 when RETURN is pressed.)

Changing I/O Offset changes the address in the format corresponding to the
programmer's Begin RAM address.  I/O Offset is entered in hexadecimal and
remains in effect until changed again or until PROMlink is exited.
%
~----------19
DEVICE BLOCK SIZE is used to set the number of bytes in the device
that will be affected by all subsequent device operations.

The Device Block Size of 0 (default) specifies that all of the device is 
to be used.  Transferring data to or from a portion of a device may be 
done by changing Device Block Size and Begin Device Address.

Device Block Size is entered in hexadecimal and may be changed to its 
default value of 0 by pressing the RETURN key.  It remains in effect 
until changed again or until PROMlink is exited.
%
~----------20
BEGIN DEVICE ADDRESS is used to set the device address that will
correspond to the Begin RAM Address for all subsequent device operations.

Begin Device Address is entered in hexadecimal and may be changed to its
default value of 0 by pressing the RETURN key.  It remains in effect until
changed again or until PROMlink is exited.

If the value is changed, the Device Block Size must also be changed to
specify the desired smaller portion of the device.  For example, if you
wanted to load or program only the upper half of a 2732 device (4K), you
would set Device Block Size to hexadecimal 800 (2K)  and Begin Device
Address to hexadecimal 800. 
%
~----------21
SET SIZE is available only when you are using a Model 121 or a GangPak.

The Set Size function allows you to define the number of devices to be
programmed with different patterns at one time.

Model 121 accepts any set size value in the range from 1 to 20.
System 19 or Model 29 with a GangPak accept any set size value in the range
from 1 to 8.

The Set Size may be changed back to its default value of 1 by pressing the
RETURN key.
%
~----------22
BIT MODE is available only when you are using a Model 121 or a GangPak.

Bit Mode allows data organized as 16 or more bit words to be programmed into
8-bit data devices.

Model 121:              8 or 16 bit mode values only.
System 19 with GangPak: multiples of 8 up to 64 bit mode values.
Model 29 with GangPak:  multiples of 8 up to 64 bit mode values.

The Bit Mode may be changed back to its default value of 8 by pressing the
RETURN key
%
~----------23
SELECT DRIVE allows you to switch to any drive you want simply by entering
a one-letter drive specifier.

A floppy drive must be loaded with a floppy disk before it can be selected
as the default drive.
%
~----------24
CHANGE DIRECTORY allows you to change to any directory within the same drive.

Enter the directory path to get to the directory you want to be in.  Refer to
you DOS manual for the format of a directory path.
%
~-----------25
COMPACT DIRECTORY lists either all the directory entries, or only those for
specified files, in a wide display format.  Total disk free space is shown at
the end of the directory list.

Input format: [d:][path]filename[.ext]
%
~----------26
COMPLETE DIRECTORY lists either all the directory entries, or only those for 
specified files.  Filesize, date and time of creation of the files are shown
in addition to the filenames.  Total disk free space is shown at the end of
the directory list.

Input format: [d:][path]filename[.ext]
%
~----------27
DELETE FILE displays the current directory and prompts for filename to be
deleted, and then deletes it.

Input format: filename[.ext]
%
~----------28
RENAME FILE displays the current directory and prompts for the original name
and the new name of the file to be renamed.  The name of the file is changed
to the new name. 

Input format: [d:]filename[.ext] filename[.ext]
%
~----------29
DISPLAY FILE displays the current directory, prompts for the filename to
be displayed, and then displays it.

Press SPACE to view the specified file again.

Input format: [d:][path]filename[.ext]
%
~----------30
SET DEFAULT DATA FILE displays the current directory and prompts for a file
name which will be shown as the current data file on the System Status block
for easy reference whenever needed.

Input format: [d:][path]filename[.ext]
%
~----------31
PORT selection allows you to change the serial port connected to the
programmer.

COM1 and COM2 refer to the serial ports for the IBM PC.  The TI has up to
four COM ports.  Consult your computer operation manual and PROMlink 
manual for more information.
%
~----------32
BAUD RATE selection allows you to set the baud rate.

PROMlink offers three baud rate settings as shown in the Baud Rate menu.
The default baud rate of 9600 baud (9600 bits/sec) is the maximum efficiency
level for data transmission.  Refer to your programmer manual for further
information.
%
~----------33
PARITY/DATA BITS/STOP BITS 

This function, referred to as "protocol", defines the method of serial data
transmission.

The options are:
     Parity can be: e for even, o for odd, n for no parity.
     Data Bits can be: 7 or 8.
     Stop Bits can be; 1 or 2.

Refer to your programmer manual and your PROMlink manual for more information.
%
~----------34
ENTER TITLE lets you define the title displayed on the first line of the
System Status block.  The title can not have more than 72 characters.
%
~-----------35
SAVE CONFIGURATION displays the current directory and prompts for the filename
in which to store all of the current configuration parameters.

If the filename you enter already exists, you may overwrite the old file or
enter a different filename. 
%
~-----------36
SELECT PAK TYPE allows to select the Pak type that you want to use with your
System 19 or your Model 29 programmer.  If no choice is made, the Unipak is
used as the default.

If you select the LogicPak, the I/O format will be changed to JEDEC
automatically.  If you select any Pak other than the LogicPak and the I/O
format is JEDEC,  Motorola Exormax format becomes the current I/O format.

PROMlink will operate if the wrong pak type is chosen, however, error messages
displayed may be incorrect or misleading.  Make sure that the Pak type is the
one desired at the start of each session, when equipment is changed, or when a
new configuration file is loaded.

%
~-----------37
CONFIGURE PORT lets you define the programmer to PC connections which are:
Port, Baud Rate and Protocol.

Protocol defines the method of serial data transmission.  It includes three
factors: Parity, Number of Data Bits and Number of Stop Bits.

Refer to your programmer operation manual for complete setup procedures.
%
~-----------38
SET ALL BOUNDARIES TO DEFAULTS sets all operations boundaries to their
default values.

Begin RAM:         set to 0
I/O block size:    set to 0
I/O offset:        set to 0 for the System 19 programmer
                   set to FFFFFF hex for all other programmers
Device Block size: set to 0
Begin device:      set to 0
%
~-----------39
FILE FUNCTIONS menu is a list of the file manipulation and associated 
directory functions that PROMlink provides. 

For help on individual menu items, select the function and hit the key F3.
%
~-----------40
Unrecognized error number received.

Be sure you have specified the correct programmer and Pak type (See "Select
programmer type" on the Configuration Functions menu), then consult your
programmer or Pak manual. 
%
~-----------41
The device type you have selected has been verified by the programmer as
valid.

The Data I/O family/pinout code and size in hexadecimal bytes of the selected
device are displayed for your convenience.
%
~-----------42
SPLIT RAM separates a block of 16-bit data in programmer RAM into two blocks
of 8-bit data.

Starting with 16-bit data, all 8-bit bytes at even addresses go to the block
of RAM the addresses of which are lower than the specified center point and
the 8-bit bytes at odd addresses go to the block of RAM the addresses of
which are equal to and higher than the specified center point.

The center point is entered in hexadecimal and can be set to its default 
value which is the RAM midpoint, by pressing RETURN.
%
~-----------43
SHUFFLE RAM is essentially the opposite of Split RAM.  It allows you to
interweave two blocks of 8-bit data back into one block of 16-bit data.

Shuffle RAM takes the data at addresses greater than or equal to the center
point and combines it with data below the center point such that the block of
16-bit data will now have data which was before the center point at even
addresses and data which was after the center point at odd addresses.

The center point is entered in hexadecimal.  It can be set to its default
value which is the RAM midpoint, by pressing RETURN.
%
~-----------44
EDIT FUNCTIONS describes PROMlink edit functions.  The edit RAM functions
allow you to modify the RAM in the programmer.

RAM may be filled with an 8-bit hexadecimal pattern or may be edited using
the full screen editor.

Split and Shuffle RAM are two commands used for rearranging 8-bit RAM data
for 16-bit programming.  This option is not available for all programmers.  

You can also access Set Operation Boundaries from the Edit Functions menu,
if you are not using a LogicPak.
%
~-----------45
At this point, contact between PROMlink and the programmer has already been
established.

You can proceed to the Main Menu by pressing the function key F1.  You may
access up to 10 different functions from the Main Menu.

You can also exit PROMlink by entering the letter 'x'. 
%
~-----------46
LOGIC TERMINAL MODE E1 lets you send commands directly to the programmer.
When you choose this option, PROMlink selects the programmer's Logic
Terminal (E1) mode, which sets up the programmer to communicate with a
remote terminal.  PROMlink then emulates a simple ASCII terminal until
you exit the logic terminal mode by pressing ESCAPE or one of the function keys.

The commands you can use and the responses you will receive depend 
on the particular programmer you are using.  See your programmer's 
documentation on using a remote terminal.  
Logic terminal mode is useful if

     (1)  you are already comfortable operating your programmer by remote
          terminal control, and you prefer to use this method rather than
          using PROMlink's menu structure, or

     (2)  you want to use a capability of your programmer that is not
          accessible from PROMlink's menus.
%
~-----------47
EDIT MODE is entered when you enter a starting address or hit the RETURN
key from the EDIT/VIEW RAM screen.

In the edit mode, a block of 256 bytes of RAM data is displayed on the
screen.  A list of edit mode command keys is displayed at the bottom of the
edit mode screen.

Note: Once you leave the edit mode screen by hitting F1 or F2 or go to another
block by hitting HOME, END, PGUP or PGDN, the current data block will be
written into the programmer RAM.  If you don't want to have the changes you
have made recorded in programmer RAM, you can press the F4 key to restore the
current data block.

A screen of help messages is displayed by hitting F3 in the edit mode
screen.  Leaving the editor by hitting the key F3 does not cause the changes
you have made (if any) to be written to programmer RAM.  If you now hit the
key F2, you return edit mode with the changes already made (if any) still
shown on the screen.
%
~-----------48
CONFIGURATION FUNCTIONS let you set all parameters you will need to define a
configuration file and save it for loading at another time, set all data entry
parameters, and configure the PC serial port.
%
~-----------49
LOAD USER FUNCTION displays the current directory, and prompts for a
predefined User Function filename.  

The message 'Operation successfully completed' is displayed if no error
has been detected during the loading process.  Otherwise a brief error
message is displayed.  If an error occurs, you can hit the key F3 to
get more descriptive error messages.

Refer to your PROMlink manual for detailed information on User Function files.
%
~-----------50
USER FUNCTIONS is a menu defined by the user.  To define User Functions,
you must first create a User Function file in the format specified in your
PROMlink manual, then load it in using the Load User Function function on
the Configuration Function menu.

If User Functions is selected before any user function has been loaded, the
message 'No User Function file loaded' is displayed instead of a menu. 
%
~-----------51
SET UP HANDLER BINNING displays a menu of six error conditions.  You can enter
a number from 1 - 5 (depending on your handler) to select the binning signals
to be given to the handler during Handler Programming for each of the error
conditions:
     Parts correctly programmed
     Bad device/Continuity errors
     Illegal bit/non-blank errors
     Programming errors
     Data verify errors
     Functional verify errors 
%
~-----------52
BEGIN HANDLER PROGRAMMING prompts for the number of parts to be correctly
programmed. Once a number (less than or equal to 999) is entered, the
programming session starts immediately.

The Begin Handler Programming operation is similar to that of Begin
Programming except that the handler automatically seats the devices,
programs them and deposits them into the appropriate bin according to the
selection made in the Handler Set up menu or specified in the current
configuration file.

Note: you should select this function before the first device is placed in
the handler socket.  Otherwise you will have to press the START key on your
programmer to let PROMlink know that the handler is ready for programming
devices.  Once the programming session has started, it continues until
     1)  the specified number of parts have been correctly programmed.
     2)  an unrecoverable error has occurred.
However, you can always stop a lengthy process by hitting CTRL Z.

If you have specified a number bigger than the actual number of devices in
the input tube of your handler, you can abort the handler programming by
pressing CTRL Z.
%
~---------53
You can access several related functions from the current menu.
For help on individual menu items, select the function, then type the F3 key.

%
~----------54
BEGIN PROGRAMMING is initiated immediately by selecting (1) from the Program
Device screen.

The programming operation can be aborted any time by pressing CTRL Z and can
be repeated by pressing RETURN.
%
~----------55
BEGIN VERIFYING is initiated immediately by selecting (1) from the Verify
Device screen.  It checks device data against programmer RAM data.  Be sure
that the current device type matches with of the device in the socket and
the correct data is in the programmer RAM.

The verifying operation can be aborted any time by pressing CTRL Z.  It can
be repeated by pressing RETURN.
%
~----------56
BEGIN LOADING is initiated immediately by selecting (1) from the Load RAM From
Device screen.  It loads the programmer RAM with the data contained in the
device in the programmer socket.  Be sure that the current device type matches
that of the device in the socket.

The RAM load can be aborted at any time by pressing CTRL Z and can be
repeated by pressing RETURN.
%
~-----------57
ENTER FILE NAME TO BE CREATED displays the current directory and prompts for
the name of the file to be created from the data contained in programmer RAM.

If the file name you enter already exists, you may overwrite the old file or
enter a different file name.

Input format: [d:][path]filename[.ext]
%
~-----------58
ENTER FILE NAME TO BE LOADED displays the current directory and prompts for
the name of the file which contains data to be loaded into the programmer RAM.

Input format: [d:][path]filename[.ext]
%
~-----------59
LOAD RAM FROM CURRENT DATA FILE is initiated immediately by selecting (4) from
the Load RAM From File screen.

The data contained in the current Data File is used to load the programmer
RAM.  

Input format: [d:][path]filename[.ext]
%
~-----------60
SAVE AND CLEAR TOTALS saves the error counts during the programming sessions
to a file named STATS.DOC in the current directory.  If STATS.DOC already
exists, the current error counts are appended to that file.  The error counts
are then cleared to zero.

The error counts include:
     Number of parts correctly programmed
     Number of bad device / continuity errors
     Number of illegal bit / non-blank errors
     Number of programming errors
     Number of data verify errors
     Number of functional test errors

The errors counts are accumulated from the beginning of the PROMlink session,
or since the time the error counts were last cleared using the Clear Totals
function.

In addition to the error counts, the configuration title, the date and time,
the default data file name, and the device type are also written to STATS.DOC.
%
~-----------61
CLEAR TOTALS clears all error counts during the programming session to zero.

The error counts are:
     Number of parts correctly programmed
     Number of bad device / continuity errors
     Number of illegal bit / non-blank errors
     Number of programming errors
     Number of data verify errors
     Number of functional test errors

For more information on error counts, refer to your PROMlink manual.
%
